# P43 — fix round, length and presentation (PRES-1, PRES-3, PRES-4, M4d, M7a, MIN-13 follow-through, M1c),
# from the independent audit of 4 Oct (B-38). Cuts and pointers, no new claims.
EDITS = [{'id': 'P43-01', 'file': 'Chapter1_Introduction_v2.md', 'old': "Norway's integration with MyHealth@EU is no longer prospective but operational, though its scope remains deliberately narrow. The pilot run by the Norwegian Directorate of Health (Helsedirektoratet) and the Norwegian Health Network (Norsk Helsenett SF) is in production at the emergency clinics (legevakter) in Bodø and Stjørdal, receiving patient summaries for citizens of other participating states, and in 2026 the project began establishing NCP-A, the contact point through which Norwegian health data will be shared abroad (Helsedirektoratet, 2026). No relevant patients had yet presented when the Directorate last reported, so the volume of genuine cross-border audit records generated in Norway is still minimal. Chapter 2, Section 2.2 gives the details. Infrastructure for receiving ePrescriptions from other EU and EEA states has been planned and developed but is not yet in use. Norway's scenario of implementing ePrescription without the corresponding eDispensation service required the approval of the other participating states and was carried as a change proposal to the requirement catalogue, approved and decided in the eHealth Operational Management Board on 12 March (Helsedirektoratet, 2026). As Norway is not a Member State of the European Union but an EEA member, EHDS implementation is being processed through the EEA Joint Committee, with Helsedirektoratet having delivered its formal EHDS gap analysis on 11 April 2025 (Helsedirektoratet, n.d.). The cross-border audit records with which this thesis is concerned are therefore beginning to be generated in Norwegian production, on infrastructure that is live rather than hypothetical, even though the present volume is small.", 'new': "Norway's integration with MyHealth@EU is operational but deliberately narrow: a pilot run by the Norwegian Directorate of Health and the Norwegian Health Network receives patient summaries at two emergency clinics, and no relevant patients had yet presented when the Directorate last reported (Helsedirektoratet, 2026; Chapter 2, Section 2.2 gives the details). As Norway is an EEA member rather than a Member State, EHDS implementation runs through the EEA Joint Committee, and Helsedirektoratet delivered its formal EHDS gap analysis on 11 April 2025 (Helsedirektoratet, n.d.). The cross-border audit records with which this thesis is concerned are therefore beginning to be generated in Norwegian production, on live infrastructure, in small numbers.", 'why': 'PRES-4: pilot details said once (§2.2)'}, {'id': 'P43-02', 'file': 'Chapter1_Introduction_v2.md', 'old': 'Fourth, the thesis frames the problem as one of information logistics, and the framing is structural rather than decorative. The reconciliation of conflicting retention regimes across jurisdictional boundaries is the same problem class that physical cross-border logistics manages through customs documentation retention, mandated destruction of restricted goods, and certified-destruction entries in the ledger. The audit log is the customs ledger of the digital consignment; invalidation is its certified destruction certificate.', 'new': 'Fourth, the thesis frames the problem as one of information logistics. Physical cross-border logistics meets a similar problem in customs, where documentation must be retained while the goods it describes may be ordered destroyed.', 'why': 'M7a: §1.4 overreach'}, {'id': 'P43-03', 'file': 'Chapter2_LitReview_v2_FINAL.md', 'old': "Its scope is set by location rather than by the permit's dataset, but its object is electronic health data, so it reaches an access log only where the log itself carries data concerning health, as an entry linking a patient's record in a disease cohort to an access does. For such logs the Regulation requires of one environment both that the data be deleted within six months of permit expiry and that identifiable logs persist for at least twelve months. The reading is contestable (the provision sits within the data-permit article, and a narrower construction confines it to the permitted dataset) and Chapter 4, Section 4.6 states the grounds on both sides and shows which results depend on which.", 'new': 'On the broad reading it reaches access logs kept at patient-record granularity, whose entries are themselves data concerning health; on a narrower reading it reaches only the permitted dataset. The reading is contestable, and Chapter 4, Section 4.6 sets out the grounds on both sides.', 'why': 'PRES-4: the reading argued once (§4.6)'}, {'id': 'P43-04', 'file': 'Chapter2_LitReview_v2_FINAL.md', 'old': 'Engaging with this critique is essential for the thesis to remain defensible; Chapter 6 returns to it explicitly.', 'new': 'Chapter 6, Section 6.4 engages it.', 'why': 'PRES-1'}, {'id': 'P43-05', 'file': 'Chapter2_LitReview_v2_FINAL.md', 'old': "A second strand of work has explored zero-knowledge proofs applied to electronic health record access control, with the most direct comparator for the present thesis being Albalwy (2026). The ZK-EHR framework uses a Groth16 zk-SNARK circuit to enforce policy compliance for cross-institutional health data sharing, with three actor roles defined as the Patient as data owner, the Doctor as care provider, and the Researcher as authorised analyst. Encrypted records are stored on the InterPlanetary File System, with on-chain smart contracts handling policy enforcement and cryptographic verification. The architecture decouples authorisation from identity disclosure, allowing a requesting actor to prove they hold valid policy-compliant credentials without revealing their identity, role, or affiliations. The framework reports an on-chain verification latency of approximately 390 milliseconds and a gas consumption of 216,631 units per verification. Albalwy's contribution occupies the cross-institutional space within a single jurisdiction; the cross-border space addressed by MyHealth@EU, with its eIDAS-based authentication chains and its operational coupling to ATNA-compliant audit repositories spanning multiple national legal regimes, is genuinely distinct.", 'new': 'A second strand applies zero-knowledge proofs to electronic health record access control. The closest comparator is Albalwy (2026), whose ZK-EHR framework uses a Groth16 zk-SNARK circuit to let a patient, a doctor or a researcher prove that they hold policy-compliant credentials for cross-institutional sharing without revealing identity, role or affiliation, with encrypted records held off-chain and verification on-chain. It works within a single jurisdiction; the cross-border space addressed by MyHealth@EU, with its eIDAS-based authentication chains and audit repositories spanning several national legal regimes, is distinct.', 'why': 'length: compressed'}, {'id': 'P43-06', 'file': 'Chapter2_LitReview_v2_FINAL.md', 'old': 'The Health-zkIDM system developed by Bai et al. (2022) provides a complementary perspective. Built on Hyperledger Fabric with zero-knowledge proof components implemented at the chaincode level, Health-zkIDM achieves throughput exceeding 400 transactions per second in Caliper benchmarking, demonstrating that decentralised identity management with cryptographic privacy guarantees is operationally viable in healthcare settings. The architecture targets identity verification rather than audit-trail erasure, but its performance evidence is informative for the design constraints of any cross-border verifiable compliance mechanism.', 'new': 'Health-zkIDM (Bai et al., 2022) shows that decentralised identity management with zero-knowledge components runs at more than 400 transactions per second on Hyperledger Fabric; it targets identity verification rather than audit-trail erasure.', 'why': 'length: compressed'}, {'id': 'P43-07', 'file': 'Chapter2_LitReview_v2_FINAL.md', 'old': 'A more recent contribution is zkFL-Health (Sharma et al., 2025), which combines federated learning with zero-knowledge proofs and trusted execution environments for medical artificial intelligence training. Clients train locally and commit cryptographic representations of their updates. An aggregator operating within a trusted execution environment then computes the global update and produces a succinct zero-knowledge proof, generated using Halo2 or Nova. The proof shows that the aggregation used exactly the committed inputs and applied the prescribed rule, without revealing any individual client update. Verifier nodes record cryptographic commitments on-chain, creating what the authors explicitly characterise as an "immutable audit trail" removing the need to trust any single party. zkFL-Health is significant for the present thesis in two respects. It demonstrates that the combination of trusted execution environments and zero-knowledge proofs can produce verifiable audit trails compatible with healthcare data. And it provides a recent illustration of the design pattern in which commitments on-chain reference data held off-chain, a pattern central to the cryptographic-erasure-by-invalidation mechanism this thesis proposes.', 'new': 'zkFL-Health (Sharma et al., 2025) combines federated learning, trusted execution and succinct proofs generated with Halo2 or Nova, so that an aggregator proves it used exactly the committed client updates, and records the commitments on-chain as what the authors call an "immutable audit trail". It illustrates the pattern on which the mechanism of this thesis also rests: commitments in the trail, data held elsewhere.', 'why': 'length: compressed'}, {'id': 'P43-08', 'file': 'Chapter2_LitReview_v2_FINAL.md', 'old': 'nor verifiability semantics, commitments and accumulator witnesses, by which', 'new': 'nor verifiability semantics, commitments and a tamper-evident log, by which', 'why': 'MIN-03 follow-through: the accumulator is not built'}, {'id': 'P43-09', 'file': 'Chapter2_LitReview_v2_FINAL.md', 'old': 'The thesis frames the audit-retention versus erasure problem as one of information logistics. This framing is not a metaphor but an analytical commitment that grounds the contribution within the disciplinary identity of Sustainable Energy Logistics at HiMolde and within an active academic literature on cross-border data movement under regulatory constraint.', 'new': 'The thesis frames the audit-retention versus erasure problem as one of information logistics, which places it within the discipline of Sustainable Energy Logistics at HiMolde and within a literature on cross-border data movement under regulatory constraint.', 'why': 'PRES-1'}, {'id': 'P43-10', 'file': 'Chapter2_LitReview_v2_FINAL.md', 'old': ' The Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development has estimated that full data autarky, defined as complete restriction of cross-border data flows, would reduce global gross domestic product by 4.5 percent and cut exports by 8.5 percent (International Chamber of Commerce, 2025).', 'new': '', 'why': 'M7a: OECD figure tangential'}, {'id': 'P43-11', 'file': 'Chapter2_LitReview_v2_FINAL.md', 'old': 'The structural analogy is precise rather than loose. A Norwegian patient summary', 'new': 'A Norwegian patient summary', 'why': 'PRES-1'}, {'id': 'P43-12', 'file': 'Chapter2_LitReview_v2_FINAL.md', 'old': "\n\nThe maritime sector provides a concrete Norwegian-context illustration of the information-logistics framing in operation. Norwegian regulations on the medical examination of seafarers are governed by the Norwegian Maritime Authority under the implementation of the International Convention on Standards of Training, Certification and Watchkeeping for Seafarers and the Maritime Labour Convention 2006. They were amended specifically to implement the General Data Protection Regulation, restricting electronic registration of medical certificates by seafarer's doctors operating outside Norway and the European Economic Area (Norwegian Maritime Authority, 2023). The amendments demonstrate that cross-border health data flow is not a theoretical question for Norwegian maritime and offshore logistics; it is an operationally binding regulatory constraint that already shapes which doctors can submit which medical certifications electronically and which cannot.", 'new': '', 'why': 'M7a: seafarer material tangential'}, {'id': 'P43-13', 'file': 'Chapter2_LitReview_v2_FINAL.md', 'old': 'The analogy is load-bearing at one specific point in the design, and the point is worth isolating because an analogy that motivates a topic without constraining a decision is decoration rather than framing.', 'new': 'The analogy constrains one decision in the design.', 'why': 'PRES-1'}, {'id': 'P43-14', 'file': 'Chapter2_LitReview_v2_FINAL.md', 'old': '\n\nNorwegian Maritime Authority. (2023). *Amendments to the Health Regulations reflecting the GDPR* (Circular RSR 2-2023, 2 June 2023, amending the Regulations of 5 June 2014 No. 805 on the medical examination of employees on Norwegian ships and mobile offshore units). Sjøfartsdirektoratet. https://www.sdir.no/en/regelverk/circulars/amendments-to-the-health-regulations-reflecting-the-gdpr/', 'new': '', 'why': 'reference no longer cited'}, {'id': 'P43-15', 'file': 'Chapter2_LitReview_v2_FINAL.md', 'old': " Round 4 also produced a correction to the thesis's own headline claim, recorded in Chapter 4, Section 4.6.", 'new': '', 'why': 'PRES-3'}, {'id': 'P43-16', 'file': 'Chapter2_LitReview_v2_FINAL.md', 'old': 'The third gap is contextual and has sharpened since the first version of this review.', 'new': 'The third gap is contextual.', 'why': 'PRES-3'}, {'id': 'P43-17', 'file': 'Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md', 'old': " Aikebaier's (2026) systematisation formalises this correspondence, establishing an equivalence proposition between a Destruction-IND security notion and the Board's unrecoverability criterion.", 'new': '', 'why': 'repetition: said earlier in the paragraph'}, {'id': 'P43-18', 'file': 'Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md', 'old': 'Chapter 5 demonstrates both propositions executably,', 'new': 'Chapter 5 demonstrates Propositions 1 and 2 executably,', 'why': 'four propositions now'}, {'id': 'P43-19', 'file': 'Chapter5_Demonstration_v1.md', 'old': '*Every figure reported in this chapter was produced by the code listed in Appendix A, executed under seed 20260915. No number here was written before the code that produced it was run.*', 'new': '*Every figure reported in this chapter was produced by the code listed in Appendix A, executed under seed 20260915. The full-cohort comparison of Table 5.7 was recorded on 28 September with the code of that date; the committed-subset run (Appendix A, A.21) repeats it with the current code and gives the same figures.*', 'why': 'stale-run disclosure'}, {'id': 'P43-20', 'file': 'Chapter5_Demonstration_v1.md', 'old': 'giving a mean payload of 1,006 bytes against 35 bytes for the constructed strings used during development. Section 5.8 reports the comparison between the two.', 'new': 'giving a mean payload of 1,006 bytes. These resources stand in for payload bytes of realistic size and structure; they are not what $p(a)$ is in either record class (Chapter 4, Section 4.3), and Section 5.8 uses them only to test that no figure depends on payload content.', 'why': 'M1c; stale 35-byte figure'}, {'id': 'P43-21', 'file': 'Chapter5_Demonstration_v1.md', 'old': 'Third, the Secure Processing Environment class violates at a higher rate than the cross-border class, fifty-three per cent against thirty-seven, despite receiving erasure requests at a *lower* probability. The inversion has a simple mechanism: the secondary pathway carries more constraints. Every SPE record faces the Article 68(12) ceiling by construction, while only forty per cent of cross-border records ever face any second constraint at all; the decomposition bears this out, with 49 of the SPE violations structural and only 4 request-driven, against all 37 cross-border violations being request-driven. The inversion is therefore not a sampling accident but the direct signature of a collision that the secondary pathway inherits from Regulation (EU) 2025/327 alone, without any data-subject action. Section 5.4 isolates it.', 'new': 'Third, the Secure Processing Environment class violates at a higher rate than the cross-border class, fifty-three per cent against thirty-seven, although it receives erasure requests at a *lower* probability. The reason is the constraint structure, not frequency: every SPE record faces the Article 68(12) ceiling by construction, while only forty per cent of cross-border records face any second constraint; 49 of the SPE violations are structural and 4 request-driven, against 37 request-driven cross-border violations. Section 5.4 isolates the structural ones.', 'why': 'M4d: rate not interpreted'}, {'id': 'P43-22', 'file': 'Chapter5_Demonstration_v1.md', 'old': 'Each names the record, the collision month,', 'new': 'Each names the collision month,', 'why': 'the Semantics I lines carry no record id'}, {'id': 'P43-23', 'file': 'Chapter5_Demonstration_v1.md', 'old': "The fifty per cent figure has a structural explanation. The collision occurs when $t_\\pi + 6 \\le t_a + 12$, that is when the permit runs six months or less: the condition of Proposition 3(ii) with $F = 12$, $L = 6$ and the ceiling anchored to permit expiry. Permit lengths were drawn uniformly from one to twelve months, so half the draws fall at or below six. The figure is therefore a consequence of the permit-length distribution; what the demonstration establishes is that the collision arises under ordinary parameters rather than contrived ones. Nor was the twelve-month horizon chosen to produce it: the collision rate scales as $6/\\pi_{\\max}$ under a uniform permit horizon, so a longer horizon attenuates the rate without altering the existence of the collision, which Proposition 1 establishes independently of any distribution. The twelve-month horizon models the remaining permit duration at the moment an access event is logged, not total project length, and a record created late in a multi-year permit has a short remaining horizon regardless of the project's overall size. No figure in this chapter depends on the rate; the thesis's claim is that the collision occurs, not that it occurs with any particular frequency.", 'new': 'The fifty per cent figure follows from the generator. The collision occurs when $t_\\pi + 6 \\le t_a + 12$, the condition of Proposition 3(ii) with $F = 12$, $L = 6$ and the ceiling anchored to permit expiry, and permit lengths were drawn uniformly from one to twelve months, so half fall at or below six. A longer horizon lowers the rate as $6/\\pi_{\\max}$ without removing the collision, which Proposition 1 establishes independently of any distribution.', 'why': "M4d: shortened; 'ordinary parameters' frequency claim removed"}, {'id': 'P43-24', 'file': 'Chapter5_Demonstration_v1.md', 'old': 'which the earlier presence test could not.', 'new': "which a test of the fields' presence alone could not.", 'why': 'PRES-3'}, {'id': 'P43-25', 'file': 'Chapter6_Discussion_v1.md', 'old': 'The corrected picture of Norwegian cross-border operations reinforces rather than weakens this.', 'new': 'The state of Norwegian cross-border operations reinforces this.', 'why': 'PRES-3'}, {'id': 'P43-26', 'file': 'Chapter6_Discussion_v1.md', 'old': ' Financial record-keeping under MiFID II remains a candidate instance on that revised test, flagged for future analysis rather than established: the practitioner literature reviewed in Chapter 2 treats that setting as a floor-against-right conflict, and whether any ceiling analogous to Article 68(12) operates there is a question this thesis has not investigated.', 'new': '', 'why': 'repetition: §6.10 states it'}, {'id': 'P43-27', 'file': 'Front_Matter_v1.md', 'old': 'Two declarations are made explicitly because the first version of this project, abandoned in May 2026, failed to make them truthfully.', 'new': 'Two declarations are made explicitly.', 'why': 'PRES-3: one statement, under Sources'}, {'id': 'P43-28', 'file': 'Appendix_C_Evaluation_Instrument.md', 'old': '*(v1, 18 September 2026.', 'new': '*(v1.1, 18 September 2026; item A1 reworded October 2026.', 'why': 'header'}, {'id': 'P43-29', 'file': 'Appendix_C_Evaluation_Instrument.md', 'old': 'Section C.1a states what is expected to change once it meets real respondents.', 'new': 'Section C.1 states what is expected to change once it meets real respondents.', 'why': 'MIN-13'}, {'id': 'P43-30', 'file': 'Appendix_C_Evaluation_Instrument.md', 'old': '## C.1a Status of this instrument, and what is expected to change', 'new': '### Status of this instrument, and what is expected to change', 'why': 'MIN-13: no C.1a'}, {'id': 'P43-31', 'file': 'Appendix_C_Evaluation_Instrument.md', 'old': 'Chapter 1, Section 1.5, Chapter 3, Section 3.8, and the Declaration of Originality', 'new': 'Chapter 1, Section 1.5, Chapter 3, Section 3.9, and the Declaration of Originality', 'why': 'wrong reference (ethics is §3.9)'}]
